<h1 style=3D"font-size:26px;line-height:1.2;">Better critique sessions</h1>
<p>Hi Anna,</p>
<p>A useful design critique starts with a shared understanding of what=E2=80=
=99s on the screen=E2=80=94not a race to suggest changes.</p>
<p>In this issue, guest contributor Oskar Heim explains how to separate obser=
vations from proposed solutions. Instead of opening with =E2=80=9CMove the bu=
tton,=E2=80=9D try =E2=80=9CThe primary action is difficult to distinguish fr=
om the secondary actions.=E2=80=9D That gives the designer a problem to explo=
re without prescribing the answer.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:19px;">Try this in your next critique</h2>
<ul style=3D"padding-left:22px;">
<li>State the design goal and the feedback you need before presenting.</li>
<li>Ask reviewers to describe what they notice, then explain why it matters.<=
/li>
<li>Collect possible solutions separately, after the group agrees on the prob=
lem.</li>
<li>Close by summarizing the decisions and any questions still open.</li>
</ul>
<p>The aim isn=E2=80=99t to remove strong opinions. It=E2=80=99s to make thei=
r reasoning visible, so the team can use them.</p>
<p>Thanks for reading,<br>The Layout Letter team</p>
